# Including the bending beam benchmark with 10% porosity and an exponential
# fluid weakening factor (27 here) tests the melt weakening law. The
# counterpart test melt_10percent_alpha0_viscoelastic_bending_beam_particles is
# identical except that the weakening factor is 0. In both models the
# permeability is negligible (1e-20, not 0, so that all cells remain melt
# cells), which removes fluid segregation from the comparison.
#
# Both the shear and the compaction viscosity are scaled by
# exp(-\alpha_\phi \phi), so at the first timestep, where the elastic
# stresses are still zero, the velocities of the two models differ by
# exactly that factor:
#     max. velocity ratio = 1.487973171e1 (exp(27*0.1) = 1.487973172e1)
# The ratio increases once elastic stresses build up (14.93 after 1000 yr,
# 16.11 after 2000 yr in longer versions of this model).

include $ASPECT_SOURCE_DIR/tests/melt_10percent_viscoelastic_bending_beam_particles.prm

set Output directory = melt_10percent_alpha27_viscoelastic_bending_beam_particles

subsection Material model
  subsection Reactive Fluid Transport Model
    set Reference permeability             = 1e-20
    set Exponential fluid weakening factor = 27
  end
end
